#### Problem 1: Insecure Credential Storage in Mobile Applications

Mobile applications traditionally store API keys or user credentials locally to enable offline functionality and reduce login friction. However, mobile app binaries can be reverse-engineered, extracting embedded credentials and enabling unauthorized API access. This creates catastrophic security risk for healthcare applications handling PHI.

**Real-World Example:**
Security researchers have demonstrated the ability to reverse-engineer healthcare mobile apps and extract hard-coded API keys. Using such keys, attackers can access patient records without authentication, potentially resulting in HIPAA penalties and mandatory monitoring agreements.

**Technical Manifestation:**
```typescript
// VULNERABLE: Hard-coded API key in mobile app (easily extracted via reverse engineering)
const API_KEY = "gw_prod_8jK3nP9qR2tY5vL7xZ4bN6mW1cF0dH"; // This will be discovered!

async function fetchClaims(memberId: string) {
  const response = await fetch(`https://api.gainwell.com/claims/${memberId}`, {
    headers: {
      'X-API-Key': API_KEY,  // Insecure: same key for all users, no expiration
      'X-Member-ID': memberId
    }
  });
  return response.json();
}

// VULNERABLE: Storing username/password in mobile app secure storage
await SecureStore.setItemAsync('user_password', password); // Still vulnerable to jailbreak/root access
```

**Impact Metrics:**
- All users sharing a single compromised API key are exposed with no ability to revoke access for individual users
- API keys cannot be scoped (all-or-nothing access); mobile app has same permissions as admin users
- Violates CMS requirement for user-specific, revocable authorization (45 CFR 170.315(g)(10))

