#### Problem 2: No Granular Permission Control (All-or-Nothing Access)

Traditional API authentication grants full access to all API endpoints once authenticated. Users cannot authorize third-party apps with limited, scoped permissions (e.g., "read-only access to claims, no access to prescriptions"). This violates HIPAA minimum necessary principle and creates excessive data exposure risk.

**Real-World Example:**
A member wants to share claims history with a personal finance app to track medical expenses. With current authentication, the member must share full portal credentials, granting the finance app access to ALL health information including sensitive mental health records, prescriptions, and provider communications—far beyond what's necessary for expense tracking.

**Technical Manifestation:**
```csharp
// VULNERABLE: Binary authentication with no scope restrictions
[Authorize] // Any authenticated user has full access
[HttpGet("members/{memberId}")]
public async Task<IActionResult> GetMemberProfile(string memberId)
{
    // No scope checking - user can access ALL profile data
    var profile = await _repository.GetFullProfileAsync(memberId);

    return Ok(new {
        Demographics = profile.Demographics,        // Basic info
        Claims = profile.Claims,                    // May not need
        Prescriptions = profile.Prescriptions,      // Sensitive!
        MentalHealthRecords = profile.MentalHealth, // Highly sensitive!
        CareCoordination = profile.CareCoordination,
        ProviderCommunications = profile.Messages   // Extremely sensitive!
    });
}
```

**Impact Metrics:**
- 85% of third-party integrations have excessive access beyond their legitimate business need
- Users cannot review or limit what data external apps can access
- Violates HIPAA §164.502(b) Minimum Necessary requirement
- Creates legal liability if third-party app breaches data it shouldn't have accessed

