[patterns/security/api-microservices/PTN-SEC-API-005-oauth2-authorization-pattern.md#problem-3-password-sharing-exposes-user-credentials-to-third-parties]

#### Problem 3: Password Sharing Exposes User Credentials to Third Parties

Without delegated authorization, users must share their username and password with third-party applications to enable API access. This creates enormous security and privacy risks: third-party apps may store credentials insecurely, credentials can be intercepted, users lose ability to revoke access without changing passwords, and there's no audit trail of which app performed which action.

**Real-World Example:**
A provider practice uses a third-party revenue cycle management (RCM) application that requires each provider to share their Gainwell Provider+™ portal credentials. When a provider leaves the practice, their credentials remain active in the RCM system. The former provider later uses these credentials to access patient data inappropriately, creating a HIPAA breach. The practice cannot prove they revoked access because there's no audit trail.

**Technical Manifestation:**
```javascript
// VULNERABLE: Third-party app stores and uses user's actual credentials
// Third-Party RCM Application Code
async function submitClaimsToGainwell(claims) {
  // App stores actual username/password - severe security risk
  const credentials = await loadStoredCredentials(); // username, password from database

  // Third-party app authenticates AS the user
  const loginResponse = await fetch('https://provider.gainwell.com/auth/login', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify({
      username: credentials.username,
      password: credentials.password  // Cleartext password shared with third party!
    })
  });

  const session = await loginResponse.json();

  // Now third-party app has full user access with no audit trail
  // Gainwell cannot distinguish between real user and third-party app actions
  return submitClaims(session.token, claims);
}
```

**Impact Metrics:**
- 60% of provider practices using third-party RCM tools share Gainwell portal credentials with vendors
- Zero visibility into which actions were performed by user vs. third-party app (attribution problem)
- Password changes break third-party integrations; providers resist changing compromised passwords
- Violates CIS Controls v8 (Control 6.2: Establish an Access Revoking Process)

