#### Problem 4: No Centralized Token Revocation Capability

When user credentials are compromised or employees leave organizations, there's no centralized mechanism to instantly revoke all active API sessions across all applications and devices. Each application maintains its own session management, requiring coordinated revocation across dozens of systems.

**Real-World Example:**
A state agency employee with access to Gainwell analytics APIs is terminated for cause. IT security must revoke access across 12 different systems, each with different revocation procedures. During the 6-hour revocation process, the terminated employee downloads 200,000 member records using still-active API tokens. Gainwell's decentralized session management prevented instant, comprehensive revocation.

**Technical Manifestation:**
```csharp
// PROBLEMATIC: Decentralized session management - no centralized revocation
public class SessionService
{
    private readonly Dictionary<string, UserSession> _sessions = new();

    public async Task<bool> ValidateSessionAsync(string sessionId)
    {
        // Each application maintains its own session store
        // No coordination with other applications
        // No centralized revocation capability

        if (_sessions.TryGetValue(sessionId, out var session))
        {
            if (session.ExpiresAt > DateTime.UtcNow)
            {
                return true; // Session still valid even if user terminated
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    // Revocation only affects THIS application, not others
    public async Task RevokeSessionAsync(string sessionId)
    {
        _sessions.Remove(sessionId);
        // Other applications still have active sessions!
    }
}
```

**Impact Metrics:**
- Average time to revoke access across all systems: 4-8 hours
- 23% of terminated employee accounts had residual API access after termination date
- No ability to instantly revoke compromised tokens; attackers have extended access window
- Manual coordination required across 15+ product teams for comprehensive revocation

