#### Variation 2: Azure AD Authorization for Workforce (Recommended for Internal Applications)

**Best For**: Internal employee applications, state agency portals, administrative tools, DevOps automation

**Characteristics:**
- Azure AD (Entra ID) as authorization server with enterprise app registrations
- Federated SSO with state agency Active Directory/SAML identity providers
- Client Credentials flow for service-to-service authentication
- Conditional Access policies (MFA, device compliance, location-based)
- Azure AD Privileged Identity Management (PIM) for elevated access

**Benefits**: Native Azure integration, enterprise-grade security features, comprehensive compliance (SOC 2, ISO 27001), seamless SSO across Microsoft 365 ecosystem

**Configuration Example:**

```csharp
// ASP.NET Core API with Azure AD OAuth validation
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer;
using Microsoft.Identity.Web;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Configure Azure AD JWT Bearer authentication
builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi(options =>
    {
        builder.Configuration.Bind("AzureAd", options);

        // Custom token validation
        options.Events = new JwtBearerEvents
        {
            OnTokenValidated = async context =>
            {
                var logger = context.HttpContext.RequestServices
                    .GetRequiredService<ILogger<Program>>();

                var userId = context.Principal?.FindFirst("sub")?.Value;
                var scopes = context.Principal?.FindFirst("scp")?.Value;

                logger.LogInformation(
                    "Token validated. User: {UserId}, Scopes: {Scopes}",
                    userId, scopes
                );

                // Additional custom validation
                if (!context.Principal.HasClaim("role", "ClaimsProcessor") &&
                    !context.Principal.HasClaim("role", "StateAdmin"))
                {
                    context.Fail("User does not have required role");
                }

                await Task.CompletedTask;
            },

            OnAuthenticationFailed = async context =>
            {
                var logger = context.HttpContext.RequestServices
                    .GetRequiredService<ILogger<Program>>();

                logger.LogError(
                    "Authentication failed: {Exception}",
                    context.Exception
                );

                await Task.CompletedTask;
            }
        };
    },
    options => { builder.Configuration.Bind("AzureAd", options); });

// Configure authorization policies with scopes
builder.Services.AddAuthorization(options =>
{
    options.AddPolicy("RequireClaimsReadScope", policy =>
        policy.RequireScope("Claims.Read"));

    options.AddPolicy("RequireClaimsWriteScope", policy =>
        policy.RequireScope("Claims.Write"));

    options.AddPolicy("RequireStateAdminRole", policy =>
        policy.RequireClaim("role", "StateAdmin"));
});

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();

// Protected endpoint with scope requirement
app.MapGet("/api/claims", async (IClaimsRepository repository, ClaimsPrincipal user) =>
{
    var userId = user.FindFirst("sub")?.Value;
    var stateCode = user.FindFirst("state")?.Value;

    // User context from JWT token used for authorization
    var claims = await repository.GetClaimsForStateAsync(stateCode);
    return Results.Ok(claims);
})
.RequireAuthorization("RequireClaimsReadScope");

app.Run();
```

